  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 235 AM MST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nOver the weekend, ensemble guidance shows upper-level\nsouthwesterly flow to briefly change as a trough system over in\nsouthern Colorado begins to slide towards the northeast over the\narea. After this, we can expect a broad ridge to follow behind\nand pass through midday Sunday. Then into the beginning of next\nweek, we can expect to return to southwesterly flow on Monday\nbefore a larger, secondary trough is forecast to slide to\nthe south of the area from the Four Corners region on Tuesday.\nWhile confidence for the evolution of the overall pattern\nbetween Saturday and Monday is medium to high (55-75%), large\nvariability within the new trough starts to show on Tuesday as \nit could amplify to the south into Oklahoma and Texas. This \ncould potentially draw the jet stream down and push the low \npressure system further south. There is low confidence (20%) \nthat the trough would amplify to the south. \n\nTemperatures are forecast to be back to seasonal averages and\nfairly consistent over this long term period. While afternoon\nhighs are forecast to stay in the 40s, morning lows will likely\nbe below freezing after Saturday as temperatures are expected to\ndrop into the 20s over the entire County Warning Area (CWA).\n\nFor precipitation, a brief shower could happen Saturday evening\nas a weak cold front passes through. While there is some\nvorticity over the Tri-State area, the main limiting factor is\nthe lack of mid- to upper-layer moisture aloft. There is low\nconfidence (20%) that showers could occur. If showers do occur,\nit is favored to be in far west Kit Carson and throughout Yuma\nand Dundy counties. On Monday evening and all through Tuesday, \nwith the freezing temperatures, there is a potential for a \nsnow/rain mix over the western half of the CWA before turning \ninto chances for snow overnight and reverting back to rain/snow\nmix for the rest of Tuesday as the temperatures climb back up.\nAlthough with the low confidence with the secondary trough, no \nsnow accumulation is expected at this point and chances for \nwinter precipitation may change as time progresses closer.",
